### Step 1: Identify the "Crib" (Guaranteed Pattern)

  In PicoCTF challenges, the text almost always ends with the sentence "the flag is picoCTF{...}".

  Look at the very end of your ciphertext:

    naf  vptr  xe  sxzqZNV{...}
     ↓    ↓    ↓      ↓
    the  flag  is  picoCTF{...}

  Breaking this down gives you 13 initial letters:

                                     Ciphertext                                    |                                   Plaintext
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
                                          n                                        |                                       t
                                          a                                        |                                       h
                                          f                                        |                                       e
                                          v                                        |                                       f
                                          p                                        |                                       l
                                          t                                        |                                       a
                                          r                                        |                                       g
                                          x                                        |                                       i
                                          e                                        |                                       s
                                          s                                        |                                       p
                                          z                                        |                                       c
                                          q                                        |                                       o
                                       Z, N, V                                     |                                    C, T, F
  ──────
  ### Step 2: Test Your Clues in Terminal with tr
  Pipe the file through tr in your terminal using the initial mappings:
    cat substution2.txt | tr 'nafvptrxeszqZNV' 'theflagispcoCTF'

  Looking at the partially decrypted output:

  1. axraezaqqp → h i g h s c o o l
      • Clearly highschool!
  2. zqgswnfy → c o [g] p [w] t e [y]
      • Clearly computer → gives g → m, w → u, y → r.
  3. efzwyxnh → s e c u r i t [h]
      • Clearly security → gives h → y.
  4. xlzpwbxlr → i [l] c l u [b] i [l] g
      • Clearly including → gives l → n, b → d.
  5. kpxeafb → [k] l i s h e d
      • Clearly published / established → gives k → b.

  ──────
  ### Step 3: Why the Challenge mentions "N-gram Analysis"

  If you didn't have the crib at the end, you would use N-gram analysis (counting 1-letter, 2-letter, and 3-letter frequencies):

  • Single Letters: f appears most frequently in this text → English most frequent letter is e.
  • Trigrams (3-letter groups): naf is the most repeated 3-letter word → English most common 3-letter word is the.
  • Digraphs (2-letter groups): nt → th, f y → er, x l → in.
  ──────
